findings.description:
The doc says the marker "can't be used to bypass the gate", because "real new work always produces" design and task files. The gate does not check that. `evaluateEntryGate` skips any entry with `entry.retroactive` "without reading any file" (`packages/core/src/introspection/reviewGate.ts:376-379`). The only bounds are three structural rules in `retroactiveRules.ts:85-124`:

1. no design or task file;
2. checked;
3. indexed before the lowest index of *other* new work (`lowestNewWorkIndex` excludes retroactive entries, lines 66-75).

Take the first new slice, with no design or task file, marked retroactive, checked, and numbered below the other new slices. It passes rule 1 (no files), rule 2 (checked) and rule 3 (nothing new is lower). The gate skips it, and it counts as complete. The "real work produces files" argument depends on process, not the checker. The Current State section says `missing-artifact` already tolerates plan entries without a design, so the checker does not require files on new work either.

Fix: either drop the guarantee or make the marker verifiable. Options: require the adoption flow's provenance (for example, a graph commit or a plan-generation record) on retroactive entries, or require design or task files on any complete non-retroactive entry. Add a test that a new, lowest-index, file-less, checked entry marked retroactive is an error.
